{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEuTVLzsbD44PPfhnRM9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bug is fixed in the code, with a regression test added, but I could not run the checks. Bash was denied in this session, so I did not reproduce the failure by running anything and have no test output to report.\n\n**Cause:** `complete()` finds the clicked task by id, then marks every task with the same *title* as done. The comment above it says this is intentional, but that is the bug. The UI sends the clicked task's id, so the title match is the only reason the other task changes.\n\n**Changes:**\n- `test/tasks.test.js`: added \"completing one of two tasks with the same title leaves the other open\". It adds two tasks titled `write`, completes the second, and expects `[false, true]`. Against the old code, both come back `true`, so it fails for this reason.\n- `app-under-test.js` and `app.js`: `complete()` now maps on `task.id === id`. The lookup of `target` is gone, and the misleading comment is removed. `app.js` is the file `index.html` loads, so the browser fix is in there too. The two files are copies of each other, so a future change to one has to be made in both.\n\n**Not verified:** I have not run `node --test test/tasks.test.js`, so I don't know that the new test fails on the old code or that the suite passes on the new code. Both points follow from reading the code. To check, run `node --test test/tasks.test.js` from the repo root. I'd expect the new test to fail before the fix and both tests to pass after it. If you re-enable Bash, I can run it and the browser check."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1116,"cache_read_input_tokens":24825,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1116},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed